    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** standing as the clear headliner. Boasting a robust 80.98 score, **Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a high-upside matchup against McGreevy, where a confluence of park factors and a plus platoon advantage allows him to maximize his stolen base potential\u2014the most critical lever for our roster composition. Joining him in the elite tier are **Yordan Alvarez** (66.84) and **Kyle Schwarber** (66.79). While **Alvarez** continues to provide high-floor production despite a neutral matchup against Hancock, **Schwarber** remains a lock in the lead-off spot against Prielipp, where the projected volume of plate appearances reinforces his high efficiency rating.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was excluding **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, who posted a 42.92 efficiency score. While his 0.385 xwOBA suggests he is hitting the ball with authority, the matchup against Seymour proves too suppressive, as the combination of park factors and the left-handed starter's skill profile drags his projection below our active starters. Meanwhile, **Junior Caminero** (65.71) earns his spot at 3B largely due to his elite batted-ball data (0.374 xwOBA), which successfully offsets the slight park-factor penalty he faces against Bradish. Conversely, **Kazuma Okamoto** remains a volatile start today (42.20); he faces an uphill battle against the sub-3.00 xERA of Schlittler, forcing us to lean heavily on his volume in the #5 hole to justify his inclusion over **Josh Smith**.\n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, particularly regarding our middle infield and catcher slots. **Trea Turner** (63.78) is a must-start for the stolen base ceiling, but his efficiency is slightly tempered by the reality of facing a pitcher who effectively controls the running game. We are also monitoring **Gary Sanchez** (56.26) and **Christian Yelich** (52.29); while they provide marginal value, their scores are buoyed by situational advantages against Wrobleski. Managers should remain wary of the **Kazuma Okamoto** and **Caleb Durbin** tandem, as their low-scoring projections against tough, efficient arms signify that these spots are the most likely to underperform if our hitters fail to generate early-inning momentum.",
